---
import stories from "../data/vision-stories.json";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import Citation from "../components/Citation.astro";
import MotionClip from "../components/MotionClip.astro";
import MediaCredit from "../components/MediaCredit.astro";
import MediaControls from "../components/MediaControls.astro";
import clips from "../../public/media/roofing-clips.json";
const walking = clips.clips.find((clip) => clip.id === "g1-roofing-walking")!;
const field = clips.clips.find(
  (clip) => clip.id === "roofing-fieldwork-crouch",
)!;
const tool = clips.clips.find((clip) => clip.id === "g1-roofing-tool")!;
const bending = clips.clips.find((clip) => clip.id === "g1-roofing-bending")!;
---

<Layout title={stories.roof.headline}>
  <Hero />
  <div class="container visual-home">
    <section
      class="section abstract-section"
      id="abstract"
      aria-labelledby="abstract-title"
    >
      <h2 id="abstract-title">A copied pose can miss the roof.</h2>
      <div class="abstract-story">
        <div class="abstract-copy">
          <p class="lead">
            A robot can copy a roofer’s posture and still stand with its feet
            below the surface, or hold a nailgun at the wrong distance. Motion
            tracking records how a person moves. It does not reliably record
            where the body is relative to the roof.
          </p>
          <p>
            So we measured the roof and corrected each motion against it. Then
            we rewarded the robot for keeping those distances while it moved,
            because a correct plan can still drift once the robot is running
            it.
          </p>
          <Figure id="overview" compact />
        </div>
        <div class="clip-with-bubble">
          <MotionClip id="g1-walking-video" clip={walking} />
          <p class="clip-bubble">
            <strong>Walking up.</strong> The G1 steps from the floor onto a padded
            incline. The overhead line is a safety tether, and the person beside
            it holds the controller. Original speed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
      <h2 id="method-title">How we trained it</h2>
      <div class="method-map">
        <div class="method-map-figure"><Figure id="framework" compact /></div>
        <h3 class="sr-only">What the figure shows</h3>
        <p class="method-bubble method-bubble-capture">
          <span class="method-badge"><span class="sr-only">Figure panel </span>0</span>
          <strong>A person goes first.</strong> Wearing a PICO headset and motion
          trackers, they perform the motion on a wooden platform set to 9°, 17°,
          or 25°.
        </p>
        <p class="method-bubble method-bubble-retarget">
          <span class="method-badge"><span class="sr-only">Figure panel </span>1</span>
          <strong>The motion moves to the robot.</strong> We extract a skeleton
          from the tracking data and map it onto the G1’s body. The pose looks
          right, but the feet and hands can land in the wrong place.
        </p>
        <p class="method-bubble method-bubble-refine">
          <span class="method-badge"><span class="sr-only">Figure panel </span>2</span>
          <strong>We correct it against the roof.</strong> A measured 3D model of
          the roof sets where each foot rests and how far the hand stays from the
          surface: 25 cm for a nailgun, 12 cm for a hammer, 18.5 cm for pushing.
        </p>
        <p class="method-bubble method-bubble-simulate">
          <span class="method-badge"><span class="sr-only">Figure panel </span>3</span>
          <strong>It practices in simulation.</strong> Reinforcement learning
          trains a policy to follow the corrected motion. Extra rewards hold the
          hand distance and keep the hand out of the roof.
        </p>
        <p class="method-bubble method-bubble-robot">
          <span class="method-badge"><span class="sr-only">Figure panel </span>4</span>
          <strong>We try it on a real G1.</strong> Walking uphill, holding a
          nailgun near the surface, moving a hammer, and bending, on a foam wedge
          with a safety hoist attached.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="field-story">
        <MotionClip id="field-crouch-video" clip={field} />
        <p class="clip-bubble clip-bubble-side">
          <strong>Why these motions.</strong> Our team filmed this worker on a house
          under construction. The low crouch, with feet on the slope and a tool near
          the surface, is the kind of posture we want from the robot. This footage
          gives context. It was not used for training.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <h2 id="results-title">What the robot did</h2>
      <div class="results-copy">
        <p>
          In simulation, correcting the motion alone left the hand holding the
          nailgun 7.86 cm off its target distance. Adding the rewards cut that to
          0.53 cm, and hammering and pushing improved the same way. A walking
          policy trained on all three pitches finished 10 of 10 runs at each one.
          A policy that never saw 25° finished none at 25°.
        </p>
        <p>
          We then ran four motions on a real G1, on a foam wedge pitched at about
          12°. Mean joint-position error ran from 27.6 mm for walking to 79.93 mm
          for hammering. That shows how closely the body matched the motion, not
          how far the hand was from the surface. A safety hoist stayed attached,
          and an operator started and stopped each run. The clips below are
          selected movements and stop before later falls or resets.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="motion-grid" id="experiments">
        <div class="clip-with-bubble">
          <MotionClip id="g1-tool-video" clip={tool} />
          <p class="clip-bubble">
            <strong>A tool near the surface.</strong> The G1 steps forward, bends,
            and lowers a handheld tool toward the pad, then starts to come back up.
            No nail is fired. Original speed.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="clip-with-bubble">
          <MotionClip id="g1-bending-video" clip={bending} />
          <p class="clip-bubble">
            <strong>Bending low.</strong> In a separate trial, the G1 bends deeply
            and brings its hand and tool close to the surface. The clip ends before
            the robot later loses its balance. Original speed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <h2 id="limits-title">What it can’t do yet</h2>
      <div class="results-copy">
        <p>
          The robot has not fired a nail, struck anything with a hammer, or
          installed a shingle. We tested three pitches in simulation and one foam
          wedge in the lab, and never a real roof. Roof edges, weather, and
          different materials are untested, and we did not measure how much the
          hoist helped.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, the robot needs to make real contact with its tools, carry
          materials, and fit into the way a crew works. That will take people who
          know control, tool design, construction, and roofing safety.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <Citation />
    <details class="research-details media-notes"><summary>Video descriptions & sources</summary><MediaCredit /></details>
  </div>
  <MediaControls />
</Layout>
